Subject: AIT:randomness and blended frog

-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

Quotes are from
>ALGORITHMIC INFORMATION and EVOLUTION (by Chaitin)
that Tim distributed. 

>It has so far had two principal applications. The first is to provide 
>a new conceptual foundation for probability theory based on the notion of an
>individual random or unpredictable sequence, instead of the usual
>measure-theoretic formulation in which the key notion is the distribution
>of measure among an ensemble of possibilities. 
etc.

>If the object being calculated is itself a finite string of bits, and its
>minimal description is no smaller than the string itself, then the bit
>string is said to be algorithmically incompressible, algorithmically
>irreducible, or algorithmically random. Such strings have the statistical
>properties that one would expect. For example, 0's and 1's must occur with
>nearly equal relative frequency; otherwise the bit string could be
>compressed.

What about other statistical properties? This appears to distinguish only
between the completely random and its complement. Are such things as mean,
variance, and bias left out? Do we keep the old population-oriented ideas
and just stick them on top of the AIT stuff?

etc.

The following seems to address the frog-in-a-blender question.

>defines an organism to be a region
>whose parts have high mutual algorithmic information content, i.e., to be a
>highly correlated, in an information-theoretic sense, region of space.
etc.
>how closely related are two strands of DNA, two cells,
>or two organisms. The higher the mutual algorithmic information content,
>the more closely related they are.
etc.

So the pre-blender frog parts have high mutual algorithmic information
content, while the post-blender frog parts do not. The relation seems
asymmetric; once you've computed 'frog' it's easy to compute 'blended
frog,' but not vice versa.

