Date: Sun, 11 Jul 93 2:24:38 EDT
From: boojum!esr@gvls1.vfl.paramax.com (Eric S. Raymond)
Subject: NANO/HYPERTEXT: Doing, not just talking.

I took a two-hour phone call from Eric Drexler and Mark Miller of the
Foresight Institute on Friday the 9th.

Drexler & Co. have long believed that large-scale public hypertext systems
will soon be a critically necessary tool for helping human societies use
technology wisely.  They argue that the explosive increase in human knowledge
and complexity of economic life will make essential public decision-support
systems that leverage both communities of minds and huge open-ended knowledge
databases.

I share this vision.  Five years ago, I proposed, designed, and largely
implemented an enhanced netnews with hypertext capabilities.  It has a
small user community now, but due to other pressures (such as the need
to pay bills) I haven't worked on or promoted it in some time.

The Institute has long known of my efforts in considerable detail, but chose
to back a different horse for reasons which seemed good at the time; the
Xanadu Project.

Drexler and Miller called to inform me that the Xanadu Project has collapsed.

After getting my update on the admittedly incomplete state of the software,
they told me that:

	(a) I've accomplished more in a few years of part-time solo work
	    than the Xanadu people did in more than a decade of full-time work
	    by several people.

	(b) They've realized that my incremental, USENET-leveraging design
	    approach was right all along and Xanadu's was wrong.

	(c) I am now the Foresight Institute's last, best hope for
	    public hypertext, and Mark Miller and some other (not yet
	    named to me) Xanadu survivors are going to put as much of
	    their time as possible at my disposal.

My final summation of the capabilities of the code I have running now was
greeted with literal applause and cheering at the other end of the line.

I expect to be returning to intensive work on TMN-netnews shortly.  That means
I may not have time for this list, starting soon.  Instead I'll actually be
*building* the Extropian future --- or one vital component of it, anyway.
I find this a tremendously exciting and rewarding prospect.

P.S.:

The flamers, prudes, and narrow-minded zealots who've been torching me for
various sorts of alleged Extropian-incorrectness are therefore cordially
invited to pound sand.  In the Foresight Institute's considered and official
judgement, *this* heretic has already done more to make your dreams come true
than all the self-righteous jack-jawing in the history of the list.

Truly, hacking well is the best revenge.
-- 

