Date: Mon, 12 Jul 93 20:44:06 PDT
From: hfinney@shell.portal.com
Subject: AIT: Configuration space changes under evolution

Derek Zahn wrote (in "compressed mode"):

> Idea partly baked, but some cases are easy: biological evolution acts
> on genetic material; configuration space is the set of genomes.  As
> population fitness increases, number of competitive genomes decreases.
> Should actually be applied to entire ecologies since fitness landscapes
> change with coevolution, but is too large to fit in the margin of this
> email message.

I've been thinking about this (at the leisurely pace appropriate for
our "seminar"), and I'm really not sure that configuration space decreases
under the influence of evolution.

Originally we had only single-celled organisms.  Photosynthesis was
discovered, multi-cellular organisms devloped.  Animals evolved which fed
on the plants.  Large organisms with organ systems evolved.  Plants moved
onto the land, followed by animals.  Flight joined walking and swimming as
a method of locomotion.

This seems to suggest a tendency towards greater diversity rather than
less.  Perhaps if intelligence hadn't evolved even more ecological niches
might eventually be exploited, with interplanetary travel not completely
out of the question.

At a smaller time scale, if you look at a single species from formation to
extinction, it's not clear that you are dealing with a steadily decreasing
configuration space.  Species are small in numbers initially, and they
may increase their genetic diversity as they spread and multiply.

I think I can understand the intuition Derek may have been using: as we
approach an optimum, we may explore a smaller and smaller space close to
that optimum.  But I'm not sure this model applies to life, since it's
not clear there is such a thing as an optimum in this case, where as 
Derek points out the "fitness landscape" depends on what other organisms
exist.

I'm even wondering if a cyclic situation could develop, in which a population
of A's could be invaded by a B, leading eventually to all B's; but then the
B's could be replaced by C's, only to have the C's be replaced by A's.  This
kind of non-transitive dominance can be seen in voting and dice, and perhaps
could apply in life as well (from War of the Worlds: people beat germs;
martians beat people; germs beat martians). 

If such a thing could happen, it would challenge the notion that evolution
inevitably leads to increased logical depth.  Like other physical processes
which repeat themselves uninterestingly (planets orbiting the sun, etc.),
evolution could perhaps fail to make continual progress.

