Date: Mon, 12 Jul 93 20:44:05 PDT
From: hfinney@shell.portal.com
Subject: NANO/HYPERTEXT: Doing, not just talking.

Eric's message reminds us of a good reason to be civil and polite on
this list.  You never know who may be in a position of influence in the
future.

As immortalists, Extropians need to plan for the long term.  The enemy
you make today may be someone whose services you badly need 100 years
from now.  Indeed, those who get off to an early start in accumulating
resources and making plans for the distant future may well include those
who possess the power in the future to make things happen.  I would not
be surprised to find that those with Extropian and similar outlooks will
eventually wield a disproportionate share of the world's wealth.

It follows that if you do have to make enemies, this list may be one of the
worst places in which to do it.

We have occasionally debated ethics here, discussing what practices are
good or bad.  Many of us lean towards a utilitarian approach, arguing that
what is "good" is what is most beneficial to yourself in the long term.
We further argue that to a large extent the practices which will bring
long term benefit are those simple principles of hard work, honesty,
and good will which we have been taught since childhood.  There is no
need to invoke supernatural powers to frighten people into cooperation;
rather, the structure of the universe will, over the long term, reward
those who cooperate and punish those who cheat and steal.

In seeking to find allies and avoid enemies here, then, I am not advocating
a cynical, manipulative "political" politeness, where we hide our true
feelings in order to curry favor.  Rather, we need to internalize the
recognition that we are all human beings who have feelings and dreams. 
Writing a withering putdown or scornfully flaming an ignorant poster may be
satisfying, but it can bring pain to others.  Developing the ability to
feel that pain, the empathy to imagine how what you are writing may be
received, will be a survival trait in the long term.   

Whether Eric's efforts succeed or fail (and I think we all have good reason
to hope they succeed), consider the embarrassment and discomfort we may feel
at the prospect of a "list pariah" going on to achieve great goals.  These
feelings should be a warning and a lesson to us.  They show that we may
not have conducted ourselves in such a manner as to maximize our chances
of a long, successful future.

